## Why This Is Asked
Designed to test practical skills in modular design, dynamic config-driven provisioning, and CI governance for multi-environment Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- wrapper module tag injection
- dynamic resources via for_each and config.json
- per-env backend keys
- CI gate enforcing prod constraints

## Code Example
```terraform
# wrapper module interface
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
output "injected_tags" {
  value = merge({ Environment = var.environment }, { CostCenter = var.cost_center })
}
```

```json
// config.json
{
  "dev": { "buckets": ["dev-app-data"] },
  "prod": { "buckets": ["prod-app-data"] }
}
```

```hcl
# backend config sketch
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "env/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate config.json before plan?
- How would you extend to include new environments without touching code?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-cloud, multi-env orchestration, and robust gating. It probes backend scoping, provider aliasing, and policy-as-code integration in a realistic production-like flow.

## Key Concepts
- Per-env Terraform backends and state isolation
- Provider aliasing for multi-cloud deployments
- Policy-as-code gates (Sentinel/OPA) for prod safety
- Wrapper modules for consistent tagging across clouds

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend.tf (Terraform S3 backend per environment)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "org-terraform"
    key    = "state/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Providers with aliases (multi-cloud)
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  alias  = "aws"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp"
}
```

```javascript
// Wrapper module usage for tags
module "shared_tags" {
  source      = "./modules/tags"
  environment = var.environment
  cost_center = var.cost_center
}
```

```javascript
// Simple OPA/rego style policy (example)
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.environment != "prod"
}
```

```javascript
// CI gate steps (high level)
terraform init
terraform plan -out=tfplan
terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json
# Evaluate plan.json against policy (opa or sentinel)
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure a tiny Terraform project with proper state backends, a reusable module, and basic CI guards.

## Key Concepts
- Local vs remote backends and state isolation
- Modules and outputs for reusability
- State locking with DynamoDB
- CI gates that validate plan scope

## Code Example
```hcl
# backend for dev (local)
terraform {
  backend "local" {
    path = "terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/main.tf
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = "private"
  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "lock" {
  name           = "tfstate-lock"
  billing_mode   = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key       = "LockID"
  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}

output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket
}

output "locks_table_arn" {
  value = aws_dynamodb_table.lock.arn
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/variables.tf
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool; default = true }
```

```hcl
# backend for prod (example)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "tfstate-prod"
    key            = "path/to/env/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tfstate-lock"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend the module to support versioned backups with lifecycle rules?
- How would you add a simple test to ensure only module-controlled attributes change during plan?
